igabit speeds and improved reliability promised by Wi-Fi 7. [Data: Entities (771), Relationships (1002,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Focus on High Throughput and Stability\",\n            \"explanation\": \"A primary objective of the IEEE 802.11be standard is not just to increase raw speed but also to deliver extreme stability and a more reliable wireless experience. This focus on stability is critical for emerging applications that are highly sensitive to latency and connection consistency. The integration of features like Multi-Link Operation (MLO), wider channels (320 MHz), and operation across multiple frequency bands, including the less congested 6 GHz band, all contribute to achieving this goal. These advancements collectively aim to provide a more robust and dependable wireless connection for users, essential for activities such as high-definition video streaming, virtual reality, and critical business operations. [Data: Entities (766), Relationships (981, 1067, 987, 1002, 1068,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Comparison with Previous Standards (Implicit via 802.11a)\",\n            \"explanation\": \"While IEEE 802.11be (Wi-Fi 7) represents the cutting edge, its development implicitly builds upon the foundations laid by earlier standards. For instance, the 802.11a standard, which operated in the 5 GHz band, was an early step in utilizing higher frequency bands for wireless networking. [Data: Entities (1002), Relationships (1426)]\\n\\nThe advancements in Wi-Fi 7, such as operating in the 6 GHz band and utilizing 320 MHz channels, demonstrate a clear evolution from standards like 802.11a. This progression highlights the continuous innovation in Wi-Fi technology, aimed at increasing capacity, speed, and efficiency to meet the growing demands of connected devices and applications. Understanding the historical context, exemplified by standards like 802.11a, provides a clearer appreciation of the signific